                "blocks": [
                    {
                        "block_id": "b_3f80fa17",
                        "type": "paragraph",
                        "text": "We generated some data = (x i,j ) 1\uf8ffi\uf8ffn,1\uf8ffj\uf8ffd1+d2 .",
                        "extracted_citations": []
                    },
                    {
                        "block_id": "b_2d9e75ba",
                        "type": "paragraph",
                        "text": "We preprocessed this data with a specified q ? = (q j ) d1 1 s.t.: 1 \uf8ff j \uf8ff d1, q ? j (x j ) = (1 ] \u2022;0.333] (x j ), 1 ]0.333;0.666] (x j ), 1 ]0.666,\u2022[ (x j )) We then went on to prespecify a regression coefficient q ?  and generated the target feature y s.t.:",
                        "extracted_citations": []
                    },
                    {
                        "block_id": "b_b9a11691",
                        "type": "paragraph",
                        "text": "This gives us n responses = (y i ) n 1 . In practice, we are just given (, ) and wish to recover the \"true\" model q ?",
                        "extracted_citations": []
                    },
                    {
                        "block_id": "b_f2c1b1f6",
                        "type": "paragraph",
                        "text": ", q ? . To do so in a finite sample setting, we give ourselves a model selection criterion like BIC, AIC, test set AUC, ... All discretization / grouping schemes q 2 Q cannot be tested (very high combinatorics) so that there is a need for additional assumptions / modelling + estimation procedure to reduce the computational burden and have at least some statistical guarantees (in n) to recover q ?",
                        "extracted_citations": []
                    },
                    {
                        "block_id": "b_3b6eaa62",
                        "type": "paragraph",
                        "text": ". N.B.: If the true data generating mechanism is not a discrete logistic regression, we get a representation of X that yields the \"best\" logistic regression (on discrete data w.r.t. a given criterion / loss), which is in practice very useful in the field of Credit Scoring (initial use case of this work).",
                        "extracted_citations": []
                    },
                    {
                        "block_id": "b_fa98f291",
                        "type": "paragraph",
                        "text": "We rely on a softmax approximation of q, i.e. q a j,h \u00b5 exp(a j,h,0 + a j,h,1 x j ), where the likelihood of (q, q) is \"swapped\" for (a, q). Refer to the paper for the proper justifications.",
                        "extracted_citations": []
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "section_id": "sec_bca363e9",
                "title": "Neural network architecture",
                "blocks": [
                    {
                        "block_id": "b_406332d7",
                        "type": "paragraph",
                        "text": "To estimate q and a, we can use shallow neural networks by stating that: -If we allow q j to take m j values (we discretize in m j intervals), then we need m j softmax neurons per raw continuous feature x j . Other features shall not be connected to these neurons; -If we wish to group x j (categorical) into m j \uf8ff o j levels, we one-hot encode each factor level and have an input neuron per level; we densely connect each of these neurons to m j softmax neurons.",
                        "extracted_citations": []
                    },
                    {
                        "block_id": "b_b0fe9003",
                        "type": "paragraph",
                        "text": "Again, refer to the paper for the proper justifications.",
                        "extracted_citations": []
                    },
                    {
                        "block_id": "b_87cad0c5",
                        "type": "paragraph",
